+The task is a competitive programming problem. It is either a complete program that reads standard input and writes standard output, or a method of a given `Solution` class. Hidden tests will grade the result, so correctness on edge cases and running time on maximum-size inputs both matter.
+
+Work through the following phases in order.
+
+Phase 1 - Understand and plan:
+- Locate the solution file or starter code in {self.git_tempdir} (use the editor/bash tools to look around).
+- Determine the required interface: stdin/stdout program, or a `Solution` class method with a specific signature. Do not change that interface.
+- Extract the constraints (n, value ranges, time limit if stated) and the exact output format.
+- Choose an algorithm whose complexity fits the constraints. Think about worst cases before coding.
+
+Phase 2 - Implement:
+- Write the solution in the expected file inside {self.git_tempdir}.
+- For stdin/stdout programs, read input via `sys.stdin` (e.g. `sys.stdin.readline` or `sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()`) and write output with a single `print`/`sys.stdout.write` at the end when possible.
+- For class-based tasks, keep the exact method signature and return the value (do not print).
+- Keep recursion shallow or use an iterative approach; do not rely on raising the recursion limit for deep recursion.
+
+Phase 3 - Test (keep all test files OUTSIDE the solution file, e.g. in /tmp/test_solution.py or in a `tests_agent/` directory; never write test code into the solution file):
+- Example tests: run every example from the problem statement and compare outputs exactly.
+- Edge-case tests: hand-write minimal and boundary inputs (smallest n, all equal values, maximum values, empty-like cases where allowed, negative numbers if allowed, etc.).
+- Brute-force stress test: write a simple, obviously-correct brute-force reference and a randomized generator of small inputs; compare the solution against the brute force over many random cases (at least several hundred) and investigate every mismatch.
+- Timing test: generate maximum-size inputs (the largest n allowed by the constraints, worst-case structure) and check the solution finishes comfortably within the time limit.
+- Run every command with a timeout so the shell is not lost, e.g. `timeout 60 python /tmp/test_solution.py` or `timeout 10 python {self.git_tempdir}/solution.py < /tmp/input.txt`. Never run an unbounded or interactive command.
+
+Phase 4 - Refine:
+- If any test fails or any run is too slow, fix the solution (not the expected output) and rerun ALL tests until they pass.
+
+Phase 5 - Final review:
+- Check the output format (exact spacing, newlines, no extra text), the function/class signature, that no debug output is printed, that no test code remains in the solution file, and that recursion depth and memory are safe.
+- Leave the final, correct solution in the repository files.{extra}
+
+Please analyze the problem description carefully, then carry out the phases above and make the edits needed to complete the task."""
+
+        msg_history = chat_with_agent(instruction, model=self.code_model, msg_history=[], logging=safe_log)
+
+        # Bounded verification loop: independent review, stress testing and bug fixing.
+        for _ in range(2):
+            edits_before = self.get_current_edits()
+            verify_prompt = f"""Independently verify the current solution in {self.git_tempdir} for the problem below.
+
+<problem_description>
+{self.problem_statement}
+</problem_description>
+
+The current diff of the repository is provided in the previous message.
+
+Please:
+1. Re-read the problem statement and check the interface, output format, and constraints against the current code.
+2. Review the current diff for logic bugs and off-by-one errors.
+3. If not already done, write a brute-force reference and a randomized stress test (kept outside the solution file, e.g. /tmp/test_solution.py) and compare on many small random inputs.
+4. Check the worst-case running time on maximum-size inputs; run every command with `timeout 60 python ...`.
+5. Fix any bug or performance problem you find directly in the solution file, and rerun the tests.
+
+If everything passes and no changes are needed, reply with the single word VERIFIED (in your final response). Otherwise, explain briefly what you fixed."""
+            history_before_len = len(msg_history)
+            msg_history = chat_with_agent(
+                verify_prompt,
+                model=self.code_model,
+                msg_history=msg_history + edits_before,
+                logging=safe_log,
+            )
+            final_response = _last_assistant_text(msg_history[history_before_len:])
+            edits_after = self.get_current_edits()
+            made_edits = _diff_text(edits_before) != _diff_text(edits_after)
+            if "VERIFIED" in final_response and not made_edits:
+                break
+
+def _message_text(msg):
+    """Extract plain text from a message in either Claude or OpenAI-style history."""
+    content = msg.get("content", "") if isinstance(msg, dict) else getattr(msg, "content", "")
+    if isinstance(content, str):
+        return content
+    parts = []
+    for block in content or []:
+        if isinstance(block, dict):
+            if block.get("type") == "text":
+                parts.append(block.get("text", ""))
+        elif getattr(block, "type", None) == "text":
+            parts.append(getattr(block, "text", ""))
+    return "\n".join(parts)
+
+def _last_assistant_text(msg_history):
+    """Return the text of the last assistant message in a slice of message history."""
+    for msg in reversed(msg_history):
+        role = msg.get("role") if isinstance(msg, dict) else getattr(msg, "role", None)
+        if role == "assistant":
+            return _message_text(msg)
+    return ""
+
+def _diff_text(msg_history):
+    """Return the diff text stored in a message list produced by get_current_edits()."""
+    return "\n".join(_message_text(m) for m in msg_history)
+
 
